---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const formatHref = toolPathBySlug('color-format-converter');
const shadesHref = toolPathBySlug('color-shades-generator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you extract colors from an image? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Choose a picture from your device. The page samples it in the tab and returns a short row of
      colors. Each stop shows a hex and its pixel share. Copy CSS writes custom properties you can
      paste into a stylesheet. There is no address box, because a remote URL would leave the device.
    </p>
    <p>
      An image color palette here is the colors that actually occupy the file, not one blended
      swatch. People type extract colors from image, get palette from photo, dominant color from image, and image to palette. They mean this page. For example, a product photo with a white
      wall and a red logo keeps both. The wall takes most of the pixel share. The logo stays its
      own stop. Pulling a logo red out of a product photo before it is averaged away is the job.
      Copying CSS custom properties from a picture that stays on this device is the thing you leave with.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Image Palette Extractor →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="cut">
    <h2>How median cut picks a palette <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      How median cut picks a palette is a split, not a blur. The long edge is sampled on a grid.
      Each sample keeps the pixel it lands on. The samples are then cut at the median in OKLab
      until the boxes are small, and boxes that sit close together are merged. That is the whole
      algorithm. It does not average a rectangle and call the result a color.
    </p>
    <p>
      A palette versus an average of the picture is the difference you can see. An average of a
      white wall and a red mark is pink, and the red is gone. The cut keeps the red as long as a
      sample landed on it. For example, three white pixels and one red pixel return white and red,
      not one pink.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="share">
    <h2>Read the pixel share on a swatch <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Read the pixel share on a swatch as the fraction of samples in that stop. Seeing which stop
      is the wall, because its pixel share is most of the picture, is the point of the number.
      A background should return several similar stops only when those stops are actually different.
      Close grays are one wall. The share on that one stop is large, and the subject is still in the row.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, a picture that is nine tenths light gray and one tenth #e10600 shows the gray
      near 90% and the red near 10%. You do not have to guess which chip is the backdrop.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gamut">
    <h2>Why a hex is marked clamped <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Why a hex is marked clamped is a gamut fact, not a second palette. Some photos are display-p3.
      A display-p3 red can sit outside sRGB. The page converts the sample, writes the sRGB hex, and
      leaves a line when a channel had to be clamped. The copied value is that clamped hex. An
      ordinary sRGB file does not show the line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not treat the hex as the camera value once that line is visible. It is the color CSS can
      store in sRGB. The <a href={formatHref}>color format converter</a> will turn it into RGB or
      OKLCH. The <a href={shadesHref}>shades generator</a> will build a ramp from a stop you kept.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The common mistake is to average a region and call it the palette. The logo red the person
      can see never appears, because it was mixed into the wall. This page refuses that average.
    </p>
    <p>
      Another miss is a row of near-identical background stops with the subject left out. Merge
      those stops. The pixel share then names the wall, and a distinct color still has a seat.
    </p>
    <p>
      A third miss is copying a wide-gamut sample as if the hex were unclamped. When the line is
      up, the hex is the clamped sRGB value. A warning on a quiet sRGB photo would be noise, so
      the line stays hidden until a sample was actually outside the gamut.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
